# Chapter 2 · From Women's Studies to Gender Studies

> The examiner asked you to "differentiate between Gender Studies and Women's Studies" in 2017, 2018, 2019 and 2023. To do it well you need the history of both fields, clear differences on named points, examples, and a view on how the two relate.

## 2.1  The birth of Women's Studies

Women's Studies was born in American universities in the late 1960s. It grew out of the **second wave** of the women's movement.

- In ==1969== **Cornell University** offered one of the first courses on women.
- In ==1970== **San Diego State College** started the first full Women's Studies programme.
- By the mid-1970s there were hundreds of courses. In ==1977== the **National Women's Studies Association** was founded in the USA.
- Britain, Canada, Australia and Western Europe followed in the 1970s.

::: case India's Women's Studies movement | the South Asian beginning
In India, a government report called *Towards Equality* (==1974==) showed how little women's lives had improved since independence. The same year, SNDT Women's University in Bombay set up a research unit on women. The Indian Association for Women's Studies was formed in the early 1980s.
-> In South Asia, Women's Studies grew from government concern as well as from activism. Pakistan followed a similar path in 1989 (Chapter 5).
:::

From the start, Women's Studies had three features:

1. **Women as the subject** — the field studied women's history, work, writing, health and experience.
2. **Women as the knowers** — research was done by women, using women's own accounts.
3. **Women as the beneficiaries** — knowledge was meant to improve women's lives. Its founders called it "the academic arm of the women's movement".

## 2.2  How and why Women's Studies became Gender Studies

From the 1980s many programmes changed their name to "Gender Studies" or "Women's and Gender Studies". Five changes caused this:

1. **The idea of "gender" grew up.** Ann Oakley (==1972==), Gayle Rubin's "sex/gender system" (==1975==) and Joan Scott's essay (==1986==) showed that the real subject was a *relationship* between women and men, not women alone.
2. **Men began to be studied.** Scholars such as Raewyn Connell (*Gender and Power*, ==1987==) showed that men are gendered too, and that "manhood" has costs as well as benefits.
3. **Queer theory arrived** (about ==1990==). It brought gay, lesbian, transgender and intersex people into the field.
4. **The single idea of "woman" was questioned.** Black women, working-class women and women of the Global South said that Women's Studies had described the lives of white, middle-class Western women as if they were every woman's life (bell hooks, ==1981==; Chandra Talpade Mohanty, ==1984==).
5. **Aid agencies changed their language.** In the 1980s they moved from "Women in Development" to "Gender and Development" (Topic 4). Universities followed the money and the words.

::: fig timeline | From Women's Studies to Gender Studies.
1969: First course on women at Cornell
1970: First Women's Studies programme, San Diego State
1974: India's Towards Equality report
1975: Rubin's "sex/gender system"
1977: US National Women's Studies Association
1986: Joan Scott: gender as a way of showing power
1987: Connell, Gender and Power — men are gendered too
1990: Butler; queer theory begins
:::

## 2.3  The main differences

::: table | Women's Studies and Gender Studies compared on eight points.
@widths 2.8,5.6,5.6
Point | Women's Studies | Gender Studies
Subject | Women: their lives, work, history, writing, bodies | Gender relations: women, men and gender-diverse people, and the relations between them
Key idea | "Woman" and women's oppression | "Gender" as something society makes, and as a relation of power
Origin | The women's movement, late 1960s | Gender theory, men's studies and queer theory, 1980s–1990s
Aim | Make women visible and end their subordination | Explain how gender is made and how it shares out power for everyone
Politics | Openly feminist and woman-centred | Feminist roots, but wider; includes men and sexualities
Whose view | Research by and for women, from women's experience | Research from many viewpoints, including men's
Theories used | Liberal, radical and socialist feminism | Adds social constructionism, queer theory and intersectionality
Institutions | Women's Studies centres (Pakistan's centres of 1989) | Departments of Gender Studies (most Pakistani universities today)
:::

::: example | The same problem, studied two ways
**Honour killing.** A Women's Studies approach puts the victims at the centre: the women killed, their families' accounts, the failure of police and courts to protect them. A Gender Studies approach adds the men and the system: how ideas of *ghairat* (honour) and manhood are taught to boys, why brothers and fathers kill, how families and jirgas bargain over "forgiveness", and how the law let killers escape until the ==2016== amendment.
-> Women's Studies asks "what happens to women?" Gender Studies also asks "what makes men do this, and what system connects them?"
:::

::: example | A second example: health in childbirth
Women's Studies records women's own experience of pregnancy, childbirth and neglect. Gender Studies also studies how husbands and mothers-in-law decide whether a woman may visit a clinic, how male doctors hold authority, and how a health budget set mostly by men chooses its priorities.
:::

## 2.4  What the two fields share

- **Feminist roots.** Both grew out of the women's movement and share its aim of equality.
- **A critique of male-centred knowledge.** Both reject knowledge that treats men as the human norm.
- **Many disciplines.** Both use the tools of many subjects.
- **An aim of change.** Both want to guide policy and improve lives.

## 2.5  How they relate — rivals or partners?

There are three views:

1. **Growth.** Gender Studies is the grown-up form of Women's Studies: the same project with a better idea.
2. **Dilution.** Critics warn that the word "gender" can hide women again. The American critic Tania Modleski called her 1991 book *Feminism Without Women* to warn that some "gender" theory was losing sight of real women's suffering. In aid work, "gender" sometimes became a technical word that avoided talking about male power.
3. **Partnership.** Most scholars now see the two as partners. Many departments are called "Women's and Gender Studies" to keep both the focus on women and the study of relations.

::: fig compare | Women's Studies and Gender Studies: two lenses on one project.
@vs +
## Women's Studies
- Puts women at the centre
- Recovers women's voices and history
- Names women's oppression plainly
- Risk: treats all women as one group
## Gender Studies
- Studies relations between genders
- Includes men, masculinities, sexualities
- Explains how gender is made
- Risk: women can disappear again
:::

::: balance | The line to take in the exam
State the differences clearly. Then conclude that the two fields are **stages and partners of one project**: Women's Studies made women visible; Gender Studies explained the system that had made them invisible. In Pakistan, where basic facts of women's lives — literacy, health, work, violence — still need urgent attention, a focus on women remains essential, inside a gender framework that also studies men and power.
:::

::: trap | Three common mistakes
- Saying Gender Studies "replaced" Women's Studies. It grew out of it, and the two exist side by side.
- Saying Gender Studies is "against men" or "only about women". It studies men and masculinities too.
- Forgetting examples. The 2018 question said "substantiate your argument(s) with examples". Give at least two.
:::
